### That the act be committed with insult or in disregard of the respect due the offended party on account of his rank, age, or sex, or that is be committed in the dwelling of the offended party, if the latter has not given provocation.

In the third paragraph we have four aggravating circumstances:
1. Disregard of rank,
2. Disregard of age,
3. Disregard of sex, and
4. Disregard of the privacy of the offended party's dwelling

#### DISREGARD OF RANK
There is disregard of rank when in the commission of the crime, the offender deliberately insulted or disregarded the offended party's high social standing in society.

#### DISREGARD OF AGE
There is disregard of age when in the commission of the crime, the offender deliberately insulted or disregarded either the minor age or the senior age of the victim.

#### DISREGARD OF SEX
There is disregard of sex when in the commission of the crime, the offender deliberately insulted, disregarded, disrespected the womanhood of the victim.

#### DWELLING
If the crime committed inside the dwelling of the offended party, it will be considered an aggravating circumstance. It is aggravating because it violates the Constitutional provision on giving respect to the privacy of one's abode. It shows greater criminality on the part of the accused. Instead of committing the crime outside, he chose to commit the crime while the victim was inside the privacy of his abode.  
What is necessary for dwelling to be considered as an aggravating circumstance is that the victim was inside his dwelling, inside the privacy of his abode. The law does not require that the perpetrator must have also entered the dwelling, he may devise ways and means to commit the crime from the outside for as long as the victim is inside the privacy of his abode.

---
### When DWELLING is Not an Aggravating Circumstance
When you say dwelling, it refers to building or structure which is used for rest or comfort. If the crime is committed inside the privacy of one's dwelling, it is an aggravating circumstance. But there are certain instances wherein even if the crime was committed inside the dwelling of the victim, dwelling is not an aggravating circumstance:
        1. When it is the offended party who gave provocation to the accused; 
        2. When both the accused and the offended party are living in the same dwelling; or 
        3. When dwelling is inherent in the commission of the crime.
In these three (3) instances, dwelling is not considered an aggravating circumstance even if the crime is committed against the victim while he is inside his dwelling.


